# The first comparison reads how long the text is, whether its characters are bytes, and where they lie. The other two ask nothing of that again.
# B3-LABEL: name="flags" role=Source
# B3: Reads:JSRopeString_fiber0
# B3: Reads:StringImpl_length
# B3: Reads:StringImpl_hashAndFlags
# B3: Reads:StringImpl_data
# B3-NOT: Reads:JSRopeString_fiber0
# B3-NOT: Reads:StringImpl_
# B3-LABEL: end of function

# A rope and a 16-bit text go to a stub, which is the only thing here that needs a frame. Whoever has gone there once goes from stub to stub: the frame is entered once, and left behind the last stub.
# The stub writes the register that `text` came in. `text` steps aside once; `found` stays in the register that it has outside.
# On the way of a flat 8-bit text that begins with `lit_`, a comparison is one branch on the two characters that are left.
# CHECK-LABEL: name="flags" role=Source
# CHECK-NOT: Frame
# CHECK: EnterFrame
# CHECK-NEXT: Move %
# CHECK-NEXT: Move $0x61615f74696c,
# CHECK-NEXT: Move %
# CHECK-NEXT: Patch
# CHECK-NOT: Frame
# CHECK: Move $0x62615f74696c,
# CHECK-NEXT: Move %
# CHECK-NEXT: Patch
# CHECK-NEXT: Successors: #[[third]], #[[same]]
# CHECK-NEXT: BB#[[same]]:
# CHECK-NEXT: Predecessors:
# CHECK-NEXT: Xor32 $2, %
# CHECK-NEXT: Jump
# CHECK-NEXT: Successors: Unlikely:#[[third]]
# CHECK-NEXT: BB#[[third]]:
# CHECK-NEXT: Predecessors:
# CHECK-NEXT: Move $0x63615f74696c,
# CHECK-NEXT: Move %
# CHECK-NEXT: Patch
# CHECK-NOT: EnterFrame
# CHECK: Load16 4(%
# CHECK-NEXT: Patch
# CHECK-NEXT: Branch32 Equal, %[[tail]], $24929
# CHECK: Predecessors:
# CHECK: Predecessors:
# CHECK: Predecessors:
# CHECK-NEXT: Patch
# CHECK-NEXT: Branch32 NotEqual, %[[tail]], $25185
# CHECK: Predecessors:
# CHECK: Predecessors:
# CHECK-NEXT: Patch
# CHECK-NEXT: Branch32 Equal, %[[tail]], $25441
# CHECK-NOT: EnterFrame
# CHECK-LABEL: end of function
